---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SurveyDesignPrinciples from "../components/SurveyDesignPrinciples.astro";
import ScienceGoals from "../components/ScienceGoals.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Survey"
  description="The MISTY Survey: a resolved view of the baryon cycle across the low-metallicity regime."
>
  <article class="survey-page">
    <div class="survey-page__intro">
      <div class="survey-page__prose">
        <p>
          How do galaxies turn gas into stars, regulate their growth, and assemble 
          their mass across cosmic time? At the heart of this question is the baryon cycle, 
          the continual exchange of matter and energy between stars, the interstellar medium (ISM), 
          and the large-scale circumgalactic environment. Gas cools and gravitationally collapses 
          to form stars, while massive stars return energy and newly synthesized elements through 
          radiation, winds, and supernovae, disrupting their natal clouds and recycling material 
          within galaxies and into their surroundings.
        </p>
        <p>
          MISTY is designed to establish a resolved view of this cycle across the low-metallicity 
          regime, where the physical conditions that govern galaxy formation and evolution differ 
          fundamentally from those in massive, near-solar-metallicity systems. Metal-poor massive 
          stars emit harder ionizing radiation; reduced dust and metal content alter shielding, 
          cooling, and chemistry; and shallow gravitational potentials allow stellar feedback to 
          redistribute or expel gas more efficiently.
        </p>
        <p>
          MISTY is designed around two defining features of the baryon cycle: it operates across 
          a wide range of spatial scales and links stars, multiphase gas, and dust. Understanding 
          it therefore demands a multi-scale, multi-wavelength approach. The formation and evolution 
          of young star clusters, H II-region structure, cloud dispersal, metal enrichment, and 
          turbulence injection occur on scales of approximately 10–100 pc, while their cumulative 
          effects on gas flows, ionizing-radiation escape, galaxy-wide star formation, and chemical 
          evolution must be measured across entire galaxies. Characterizing stars, multiphase gas, 
          and dust further requires observations across the electromagnetic spectrum. 
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="survey-page__principles">
      <SurveyDesignPrinciples />
    </div>

    <div class="survey-page__science-goals">
      <ScienceGoals />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .survey-page {
    max-width: var(--reading-max);
    /* Combined with .site-main padding-top (~1.5rem): ~44–48px total */
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .survey-page__prose {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.68;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .survey-page__prose p {
    margin-bottom: 1.15em;
  }

  .survey-page__prose p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .survey-page__principles {
    margin-top: 3rem; /* ~48px mobile separation */
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  .survey-page__science-goals {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .survey-page {
      /* Combined with .site-main padding-top (~1.85rem): ~56px total */
      padding-top: 1.65rem;
    }

    .survey-page__prose {
      font-size: 1.125rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
    }

    .survey-page__principles {
      margin-top: 4.5rem; /* ~72px desktop separation */
    }
  }
</style>
